# Repositories the g-cauc runner pool serves, one VM each (gcauc-<repo>):
#   owner/repo  slots  CPUs  memory GiB
# Slots are the most jobs at once, not a reservation. A repo always runs 1 job at a time; each
# further slot starts only while the Mac has room (see slot in gcauc-runner), so CI grows into a
# free Mac and backs off while Garrett's own work needs it. CPUs are shared with the Mac and the
# other VMs (a quiet VM uses almost none); memory is the VM's own, so the total must leave the Mac
# room (64 GiB, 2026-10-05). Each slot gets CPUs / slots of its VM's CPUs.
# 2026-10-09: Bison Brain went from 2 slots to 4 (18 runs waited; a job used about 1.5 of its 3
# CPUs and up to 3 GiB), and myqap from 2 to 4, so the 4 jobs of a myqap run start together.
# The Mac has 6 performance and 12 efficiency cores. When all VMs are busy, each gets a share in
# proportion to its CPUs, so CallFlow (the longest suite) gets the most; myqap's 2 CPUs per slot
# match GitHub's runners (2026-10-05: CallFlow end-to-end hit its 30-minute limit at 8+8+12).
# Add a line when a project adopts g-cauc (the adopt skill), then run `gcauc-runner up` and
# restart the agent with no job running (launchctl kickstart -k gui/$(id -u)/com.gcauc.runner).
garrettyarmo/callflow 2 12 12
garrettyarmo/bisonbrain 4 12 12
garrettyarmo/myqap 4 8 10
garrettyarmo/lrcp-ops 1 2 4
